# Force LF line endings for files that run as scripts inside Linux
# containers, regardless of the checkout platform's core.autocrlf setting.
#
# Without this, a Windows checkout with the common core.autocrlf=true
# converts LF to CRLF on checkout -- which silently breaks any POSIX shell
# script mounted into a container (a CRLF-corrupted `set -eu` line becomes
# `set -eu\r`, which `sh` rejects with "illegal option -", failing the
# script). Confirmed empirically: examples/n8n/scripts/n8n-init.sh, checked
# out fresh with core.autocrlf=true, fails compose.dev.yml's `n8n-init`
# step this exact way. See tests/test_repo_hygiene.py for the regression
# guard that checks tracked shell scripts never contain CRLF.
*.sh text eol=lf

# P2.1 Wave 1 A5 revision round 1: the same class of bug, discovered by the
# required "self-contained CI-equivalent run from a clean repository-only
# checkout" verification. Sync-P2.1Schemas.ps1/Sync-ActivityBinding.ps1/
# generate-evidence-validators.mjs all write these files directly via raw
# byte I/O (bypassing any git checkout filter), always LF -- but a genuine
# `git clone` on a machine with the common Windows core.autocrlf=true
# converts the committed LF bytes to CRLF on checkout, changing the file's
# SHA-256 and breaking every hash pinned against it (schema_provenance.json/
# validator_provenance.json's sourceSha256, the activity binding lockfile's
# provenance.sourceSha256) -- confirmed empirically: -CheckOnly and the
# generated-validator drift check both failed this exact way on a fresh
# Windows clone before this rule was added. These files must reach every
# checkout byte-identical to what -Regenerate/the generator actually wrote.
examples/n8n/vendor/**/*.json text eol=lf
examples/n8n/activity_binding.invoice_intake.json text eol=lf
examples/n8n/generated/*.js text eol=lf
examples/n8n/generated/*.json text eol=lf

# Same gap, found the same way: examples/n8n/schemas/ is also hashed into
# validator_provenance.json's sourceSha256 (result-bundle-v1.0.0.schema.json)
# but wasn't covered by the rule above, so a Windows checkout still silently
# CRLF-corrupted it and produced a sourceSha256 that only matched a Windows
# author's own local regeneration -- not the LF bytes CI actually hashes.
examples/n8n/schemas/*.json text eol=lf
